CAT = "rbtDoc"; SEG = "SDOC"
Q = [
# E.1 communicating concerns and suggestions from the team
("At pickup, a client's mother says the bedtime routine the team taught has stopped working and she wants to try a reward chart instead. The supervisor visits on Friday. What should the RBT do?",
 "Tell the supervisor about it today", ["Help her set up the reward chart now", "Hold it for the supervisor's Friday visit", "Tell her to keep using the plan as written"],
 "Caregiver concerns go to the supervisor in a timely manner, so the RBT relays it now rather than waiting for the visit. Helping design a new chart changes the plan without the supervisor."),
("A classroom teacher tells the RBT that the client's hitting has spread from recess to lunch and asks whether the plan should cover lunch too. The best response is:",
 "Relay it to the supervisor to decide", ["Start running the plan at lunch too", "Explain that the plan covers recess only", "Suggest the teacher use time-out at lunch"],
 "Extending a plan to a new setting is a clinical decision. The RBT thanks the teacher, relays the concern promptly and lets the supervisor decide."),
("A speech therapist emails the RBT a suggestion to pair picture cards with every vocal request in the client's program. What should the RBT do with the suggestion?",
 "Forward it to the supervisor", ["Add the cards at the next session", "Reply that ABA does not use cards", "Try the cards on a few trials first"],
 "Suggestions from other providers are passed to the supervisor, who decides whether to change the program. Trying it on a few trials is still changing the plan without direction."),
("A grandfather who often brings the client says he is worried the client is being pushed too hard and comes home exhausted. The RBT's best step is to:",
 "Tell the supervisor his concern", ["Shorten the sessions starting today", "Explain why the current pace is correct", "Ask the parents whether he is right"],
 "Concerns from any caregiver are relayed to the supervisor in a timely manner. Shortening sessions or defending the plan are decisions the RBT does not make alone."),
("A parent texts the RBT on Saturday that the client bit a sibling hard enough to break skin. The next session is Monday. What should the RBT do?",
 "Report it to the supervisor now", ["Bring it up at Monday's session", "Tell the parent to add a time-out", "Log it on Monday's data sheet only"],
 "A serious new safety concern is shared with the supervisor right away, following workplace policy. Waiting until Monday delays a timely clinical response."),
("A father asks the RBT to stop the token board because he thinks it bribes his son. The plan was written with his consent last month. The RBT should:",
 "Share his concern with the supervisor", ["Stop the token board as he requested", "Keep it and explain it is not bribery", "Fade the tokens out over a few sessions"],
 "Caregiver concerns about a procedure go to the supervisor, who can discuss it with the father. Stopping, defending or fading the board are all plan decisions."),
("A teacher mentions that the client has started covering his ears and leaving the room during fire drill practice. How should the RBT handle this?",
 "Tell the supervisor what was reported", ["Begin a sound tolerance program", "Ask the teacher to skip the fire drills", "Prompt him back into the room each drill"],
 "New behavior reported by a teacher is passed to the supervisor in a timely manner. Starting a program or asking for changes at school goes beyond the RBT's role."),
# E.2 seeking and prioritizing clinical direction
("Halfway through a session, the RBT sees that the new data sheet has no place to record prompt level, which the plan requires. The best step is to:",
 "Ask the supervisor how to record it", ["Stop recording prompt level for today", "Make up her own codes for prompt level", "Add the prompt levels later from memory"],
 "A data recording problem is a reason to seek clinical direction promptly. Inventing codes or recording later from memory makes the data unreliable."),
("An RBT has three questions for her supervisor: a new self-injury pattern, a missing data sheet and an upcoming schedule change. Which should she raise first?",
 "The new self-injury pattern", ["The missing data sheet", "The upcoming schedule change", "Whichever came up first"],
 "Client safety comes first when prioritizing what to bring to the supervisor. Data and scheduling matters follow."),
("An RBT's supervisor is unreachable during a session in which the client's aggression is far above anything in the plan. What is the best next step?",
 "Follow the chain of command", ["Wait for the supervisor's reply", "Try a new procedure on her own", "Ask the parent what to do"],
 "When the supervisor cannot be reached, the RBT follows the workplace chain of command to get clinical direction. Waiting or improvising leaves a safety issue unaddressed."),
("For two weeks an RBT's mastered-target probes show 100% while the same skills fall apart with other staff. What should she do?",
 "Report the data irregularity", ["Keep probing as written", "Lower her scoring standard", "Retest with other staff herself"],
 "Data that do not match across staff is a data irregularity, which the RBT brings to the supervisor for direction. Changing her scoring or testing plans on her own is not her call."),
("An RBT is told to start a new extinction procedure tomorrow but has only read the plan and never practiced it. The best step is to:",
 "Ask the supervisor for training", ["Start it and ask questions afterward", "Watch a training video and then start", "Have a coworker show her before session"],
 "Training needs are taken to the supervisor before running a procedure. A coworker's quick demonstration is not training verified by the supervisor."),
("A lead RBT on site tells a new RBT to change a client's reinforcer, but the supervisor's plan names a different one. The new RBT should:",
 "Check with the supervisor first", ["Follow the lead RBT's change", "Use both reinforcers to be safe", "Ignore the lead RBT quietly"],
 "Clinical direction comes from the supervisor, so the RBT confirms before changing anything. Quietly ignoring the lead also skips the step of seeking direction."),
("An RBT notices that her interval data and her coworker's for the same session agree only about half the time. The best response is to:",
 "Bring the mismatch to the supervisor", ["Average the two sets of data together", "Keep whichever data set looks more stable", "Rewrite the behavior definition together"],
 "Low agreement between observers is a data irregularity that needs the supervisor's direction. Rewriting the definition together changes the plan without the supervisor."),
# E.3 reporting variables that may affect progress
("A client's mother mentions that his ADHD medication dose was doubled two days ago. When should the RBT tell the supervisor?",
 "Today, as soon as possible", ["At the next supervision meeting", "Only if behavior changes", "After two weeks of data"],
 "Medication changes can affect behavior and should be reported in a timely manner. Waiting for a change in behavior or a meeting delays information the supervisor needs."),
("A client's parents tell the RBT they have separated and the client now splits his week between two homes. This information should be:",
 "Reported to the supervisor promptly", ["Kept private as a family matter", "Noted only if behavior gets worse", "Shared with the client's teacher too"],
 "A major change at home might affect client progress, so it is reported to the supervisor promptly. Sharing it with the teacher is not the RBT's call."),
("A client has missed five of the last eight sessions because of transportation problems. What should the RBT do?",
 "Document it and tell the supervisor", ["Add extra trials to catch him up", "Remind the family that attendance matters", "Wait a few weeks to see if it continues"],
 "Missed sessions are a variable that can affect progress and should be documented and reported. Adding trials changes the plan without direction."),
("A client arrives yawning and says he was awake most of the night because of a storm. Why should the RBT note this in her session report?",
 "Lack of sleep may affect responding", ["Sleep loss changes the session length", "It may mean the home sleep plan failed", "The parents must sign any health note"],
 "Sleep loss is a variable that can change how a client responds that day. Reporting it helps the supervisor read the session data correctly."),
("A client's school switched him to a new classroom with a different teacher this week. What is the best reason to report it to the supervisor?",
 "It might affect his progress", ["It ends the school program", "It changes his billing code", "It requires a new consent form"],
 "A change in staff and setting is a variable that might affect client progress, so it is reported in a timely manner."),
("During a session a client keeps rubbing his ear and crying, which he has never done before. Besides following illness policy, the RBT should:",
 "Report it to the supervisor that day", ["Put the crying on extinction for now", "Note it only if it happens again", "Tell the parent it is attention-seeking"],
 "A possible illness is a variable that can affect behavior and progress, so it is reported promptly. Treating it as attention behavior is a guess about function."),
("A family says a new baby will be born next month and the client will stay with relatives for two weeks. The RBT's best step is to:",
 "Tell the supervisor about the change", ["Plan extra sessions for after the trip", "Start a new sibling program early", "Say nothing until the baby arrives"],
 "Upcoming schedule and family changes are reported in a timely manner so the supervisor can plan. Planning sessions or programs is the supervisor's role."),
# E.4 objective communication of what occurred
("An RBT is writing up a session in which a client threw toys after being told to clean up. Which sentence belongs in her note?",
 "Threw 3 blocks at the wall at cleanup", ["Got angry at cleanup and threw blocks", "Threw blocks to escape the cleanup task", "Had a rough day and threw some blocks"],
 "An objective note describes what was seen and counted. Saying he was angry or wanted to escape is an interpretation, not an observation."),
("A parent asks the RBT why her son had a hard session. Which reply stays objective and within her role?",
 "He cried for 6 minutes at cleanup", ["He was testing his limits today", "He is frustrated by the new program", "He has seemed sad since the family move"],
 "Describing what happened, in observable terms, is objective. Guesses about testing limits or feelings are interpretations and clinical judgments."),
("An RBT forgot to start the timer for one 10-minute interval block. What should her note show for that block?",
 "That the block was not recorded", ["An estimate of the block from memory", "Yesterday's data copied for that block", "Nothing, the block is simply left out"],
 "Notes must be accurate and complete, so the gap is stated plainly. Estimating or leaving it out silently misrepresents the session."),
("A session ended 20 minutes early because the client's parent arrived to pick him up. What should the session note show?",
 "The actual start and end times", ["The scheduled start and end times", "The scheduled times with a comment", "Only the time spent on programs"],
 "Documentation must match what actually occurred, as legal and workplace rules require. Listing the scheduled times would be inaccurate even with a comment."),
("A client fell from a chair and bumped her head during a session. Which is the best entry for the incident report?",
 "Fell at 2:15, red mark on forehead", ["Clumsy fall, but she seemed fine after", "Minor accident with no lasting harm", "Fell because she was not being careful"],
 "An incident report states what happened, when and what was observed. Words like clumsy or not careful are judgments, not observations."),
("After a session, the RBT's supervisor asks by phone how the new matching program went with the client. Which summary is most objective?",
 "7 of 10 correct, 2 of them prompted", ["She is really getting the hang of it", "It went really well overall today", "She did better than she did last week"],
 "Reporting trial counts and prompts gives the supervisor objective information. General impressions do not show what actually happened."),
("An RBT realizes after submitting a session note that she entered the wrong client's data. What should she do?",
 "Correct it per policy, tell supervisor", ["Fix it quietly in the next session note", "Leave it alone since it was submitted", "Delete the note and write a new one"],
 "Errors in documentation are corrected following workplace correction rules and reported. Deleting a submitted note or hiding the fix can break record rules."),
]
